sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-21> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Infinite-time questions about complex systems are almost always undecidable" ;
    dct:description "For almost any system whose behavior seems complex, almost any non-trivial question about its behavior after infinitely many steps (e.g. whether a pattern dies out, becomes repetitive, or ever produces a given arrangement) is undecidable, and such undecidability should be widespread in nature." ;
    delphi:context "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Undecidability and Intractability” (from p. 753), p. 755. Scope: systems with complex behavior, including simple programs and natural systems" ;
    dct:type "mathematical" .
}

